          "jurisdiction": "US-WA",
          "authority": "Washington State Department of Ecology",
          "status": "statutory limit",
          "metal_species": "Pb",
          "value_ug_kg": "1000",
          "basis": "finished cosmetic as sold; lead present at 1 ppm (1000 ug/kg) or above, including as an impurity",
          "product_scope": "cosmetic products (RCW 69.04.011 cosmetic definition) sold in Washington State; OTC drug products such as sunscreens, antiperspirants, diaper skin-protectants and depilatories, and non-cosmetic articles such as tattoo inks, are outside the cosmetic scope",
          "regulation_page": "regulations/washington-tfca-cosmetics-pb-1ppm",
          "url": "https://app.leg.wa.gov/RCW/default.aspx?cite=70A.560",
          "notes": "RCW 70A.560.020, effective 2025-01-01. The 1 ppm prong is a flat presence threshold not limited to intentionally added lead; WA Dept of Ecology WAC 173-339 rulemaking implements the contaminant reading (a feasibility-based impurity limit is under consideration, but 1 ppm is the in-force statutory maximum). Cadmium and arsenic are not restricted under the TFCA (Children's Safe Products Act, RCW 70A.430, governs those); mercury under the TFCA is intentionally-added-only with no numeric ppm cap.",
          "basis_class": "",
          "soy_class": "",
